---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
const headline = 'Circle Launches Arc Mainnet With Network Fees Paid in USDC';
const title = `${headline} — Stable or Gone`;
const description = 'Circle launched Arc public mainnet on September 16 with USDC natively supported and network fees paid in USDC. SOG separates the infrastructure change from USDC lifecycle status.';
const canonicalPath = '/articles/circle-arc-mainnet-usdc-gas/';
const jsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Article',
  headline,
  description,
  url: `https://www.stableorgone.com${canonicalPath}`,
  inLanguage: 'en',
  datePublished: '2026-09-20',
  dateModified: '2026-09-20',
  isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: 'Stable or Gone', url: 'https://www.stableorgone.com/' },
  about: ['USDC', 'Circle', 'Arc', 'stablecoins', 'network fees', 'gas', 'Layer 1', 'stablecoin infrastructure']
};
---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  headline={headline}
  sectionLabel="Ecosystem infrastructure"
  deck="Arc public mainnet went live on September 16 with USDC built into both the network's asset environment and its fee model. Users pay network fees in USDC rather than maintaining a separate volatile gas token, making a stablecoin part of the chain's core transaction mechanics."
  publishedAt="September 20, 2026"
  treatment="Canonical event + non-canonical analysis"
  scope="Arc mainnet / USDC native deployment / USDC-denominated network fees"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">What launched on September 16</div>
    <p><strong>Circle launched Arc public mainnet on September 16, 2026.</strong> Circle describes Arc as an open Layer 1 blockchain built for financial markets, real-time money movement and agentic economic activity. The launch announcement says Arc went live with more than 100 applications and more than 100 institutional and ecosystem builders.</p>
    <p>For Stable or Gone, the important stablecoin-specific facts are narrower: USDC is natively supported on Arc, and Arc network fees are payable in USDC.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">USDC is not just an asset on Arc</div>
    <p>Most blockchains require users to hold a separate native asset to pay transaction fees. A user can therefore have enough stablecoins to make a payment and still be unable to submit the transaction because the wallet lacks the chain's gas token.</p>
    <p>Arc changes that user-facing requirement. Circle's launch material states that fees are paid in USDC and that no volatile native token is required for gas. Circle's current USDC product page also lists Arc among the networks where USDC is natively supported.</p>
    <p>This makes the Arc launch more material to SOG than a routine third-party integration: USDC is part of the network's transaction-fee mechanics as well as an asset deployed on the network.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">What “gas in USDC” does — and does not — mean</div>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Current treatment</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Arc public mainnet is live</td><td>Confirmed by Circle on September 16, 2026.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USDC is natively supported on Arc</td><td>Confirmed by Circle's USDC network list and Arc launch material.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arc network fees can be paid in USDC</td><td>Confirmed. Circle describes Arc as using USDC-denominated network fees.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Users need a separate volatile native gas token for Arc transactions</td><td>No. Circle explicitly presents the fee model as removing that requirement.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>USDC backing or redemption changed because Arc launched</td><td>No such change is established by the launch. Deployment and fee usage are separate from reserve and redemption state.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arc's ARC token replaces USDC for network fees</td><td>No. Circle says network fees remain payable in USDC.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">The ARC token does not change the current fee model</div>
    <p>Circle also disclosed that it completed the genesis mint of 10 billion ARC tokens during mainnet launch week. The company describes ARC as a possible future coordination mechanism for security, utility and governance as Arc explores a transition from Proof of Authority toward Proof of Stake in 2027.</p>
    <p>That disclosure needs two qualifications. First, Circle says the genesis mint is <strong>not a commitment to publicly launch ARC</strong>. Second, Circle says network fees remain payable in USDC. SOG therefore does not treat the ARC mint as evidence that Arc has replaced USDC with a new gas asset.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">Who is securing Arc at launch</div>
    <p>Circle announced a founding validator cohort that includes BlackRock, DTCC, Galaxy, Global Payments, ICE, Mastercard, MoneyGram, SBI Group, Standard Chartered, Sumitomo Corporation and Visa, alongside Circle.</p>
    <p>This is relevant to the network model, but it should not be confused with a fully permissionless validator set at launch. Circle's mainnet material describes the current validator set as permissioned while presenting a longer-term roadmap toward Proof of Stake.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">Why this matters for stablecoin infrastructure</div>
    <p>The key change is not that another blockchain can hold USDC. USDC already exists across many networks. Arc makes a stablecoin the fee asset at the base transaction layer, so the same dollar-denominated asset can be both the value being transferred and the asset used to pay for execution.</p>
    <p>That can simplify wallets, payments and application flows because users do not need to acquire a second token solely to move USDC. It also makes fee costs easier to express in dollar terms. The practical effect will still depend on wallet support, application integration, liquidity, bridging and the network's operating record.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">Stable or Gone treatment</div>
    <p><strong>Current SOG treatment: canonical USDC chain-expansion event plus a non-canonical infrastructure article.</strong></p>
    <p>SOG records Arc as an active issuer-native USDC deployment and records the September 16 mainnet launch as a chain-expansion event. The event has no USDC lifecycle-status effect: it does not by itself change USDC reserves, redemption access, legal status or the recovered status of historical events.</p>
    <p>The article layer goes further by explaining the network-fee model, validator structure and ARC-token distinction. Those analytical points do not create additional canonical asset-state changes.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">What is not evidence for the USDC record</div>
    <p>Third-party launchpad tokens, short-term token price moves, referral promotions and wallet marketing around the Arc launch are not evidence about USDC backing, redemption or lifecycle status. SOG does not use those promotional or speculative claims to alter the USDC record.</p>
    <p>Wallet-level Arc support can be relevant to a separate wallet-lifecycle registry, but it is not required to establish Circle's Arc mainnet launch or USDC's native network support.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">What SOG will watch next</div>
    <ul>
      <li>an official Arc/USDC contract or deployment identifier suitable for adding to the canonical deployment record;</li>
      <li>changes to the USDC fee model or the role of ARC in transaction fees;</li>
      <li>the planned transition from the current validator model toward Proof of Stake;</li>
      <li>material Arc incidents that affect USDC transfer, redemption, bridge access or operational availability;</li>
      <li>future Circle changes to Arc's native stablecoin support.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="panel registry">
    <div class="bar">Sources</div>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://www.circle.com/pressroom/circle-launches-arc-mainnet-an-economic-operating-system-for-the-internet" rel="noreferrer">Circle, September 16, 2026 — Circle Launches Arc Mainnet, an Economic Operating System for the Internet</a> — mainnet launch, USDC fee model, day-one ecosystem scale and ARC roadmap.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.circle.com/usdc" rel="noreferrer">Circle — USDC</a> — current list of networks where USDC is natively supported, including Arc as of September 16, 2026.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.circle.com/pressroom/circle-announces-founding-validator-cohort-and-major-integrations-for-arc-ahead-of-september-16-mainnet-launch" rel="noreferrer">Circle, August 5, 2026 — Founding Validator Cohort and Major Integrations for Arc</a> — founding validator names and pre-launch integration context.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</ArticleLayout>
